timestamps again — the last heartbeat was at 1778975171553, which converts to May 28 23:46 UTC, so from 23:46 to 00:30 is only 44 minutes. I need to find the starting timestamp from the system messages to calculate the proper elapsed time. Looking at the timestamp conversion, I'm realizing the math doesn't quite add up—the last heartbeat at 23:45 was around 746 minutes, but 45 minutes later should put us around 791 minutes, not 1085. Let me reconsider whether my minute calculations are off or if there's a gap in the heartbeat data I'm not accounting for.\n\nGiven this cron job runs every 5 minutes and the previous heartbeat was at 23:45 UTC, there's clearly been a missed interval somewhere, possibly due to system downtime. I'll just proceed with the 00:30 UTC heartbeat using the timestamps I have and calculate the elapsed time properly from the session start at May 28 15:05 UTC to the current May 29 00:30 UTC.\n\nNow I'm calculating the epoch milliseconds for both timestamps to determine the progress percentage. With the start at 1778871900000 and current at 1778941800000, that's 69900000 milliseconds or 69900 seconds elapsed, which translates to 1165 minutes out of 2660 total minutes—putting us at approximately 43.8% progress.\n\nI realize the C轨 percentage should be based on actual elapsed time rather than a fixed formula. Since this is a cron job firing at 00:30 UTC, I'll include the precise epoch timestamps in the heartbeat message rather than trying to reconcile whether the previous value was 791 or 1085 minutes—I'll go with what the elapsed time calculation actually shows. I need to recalculate this more carefully. From May 28 15:05 to May 29 00:30 is 9 hours 25 minutes, which equals 565 minutes—not the 791 I initially thought. Working through the full 24-hour cycle: 8 hours 55 minutes remaining on May 28 (535 minutes) plus 30 minutes into May 29 gives 565 minutes total elapsed. But I should verify this by checking the actual heartbeat data rather than relying